message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
title: "mfa-engine: a reusable dynamic material flow analysis engine linked to critical raw material trade risk"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Khan
    given-names: Ibtisam Ahmed
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-8155-9585"
version: 1.0.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22944494
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/ibtisamkhan96/mfa-engine"
url: "https://mfa-engine-production.up.railway.app/"
abstract: >-
  A reusable dynamic material flow analysis (dMFA) engine covering the three
  standard driving modes: register-based, inflow-driven and stock-driven. It is
  applied to Denmark's wind turbine fleet (from the national turbine register),
  the global electric vehicle fleet and global data centres, and tracks inflow,
  in-use stock, end-of-life outflow and recovery for materials including copper,
  lithium, nickel, cobalt, manganese, graphite, steel and the rare earths
  neodymium, praseodymium, dysprosium and terbium, with a mass balance checked
  every year. Projected recovery is linked to 2023 UN Comtrade bilateral trade data
  from crm-trade-network for supply concentration (HHI), a linear-threshold
  cascade model with a failure-threshold sweep, and a linear program for the
  minimum trade reallocation that meets the EU Critical Raw Materials Act's 65%
  single-country benchmark. Includes an interactive Streamlit dashboard.
keywords:
  - dynamic material flow analysis
  - critical raw materials
  - supply chain resilience
  - trade network
  - wind energy
  - electric vehicles
  - data centres
  - recycling
